markdown <<MARKDOWN
# Introducing Arrays 

#### When you build web apps, you often need to keep track of multiple related pieces of information, like a list of items, scores, or usernames. Instead of creating a separate variable for each item, we can create an **array.**



<div style="background-color: #B7E0E5; border-radius: 10px; padding: 15px; margin: 20px auto; max-width: 85%; box-sizing: border-box; word-wrap: break-word; overflow-wrap: break-word;">
<p style="font-size: 14pt; margin-top: 12px; line-height: 120%;">📖 An <strong>Array</strong> is a special kind of variable that stores an ordered list of related values (such as numbers, words, or images).
</p>
</div>

---

<img src="https://images.code.org/54347b8b347d0bc1c0ef7c83776a81bc-image-1765299269132.png" alt="example arrays" width="650px" style="display: block; margin-left: auto; margin-right: auto;border-radius:10px; ">

#### Think of it like a container that holds multiple items in a specific order.


- #### Each item has a position number called an **index**.
- #### Index numbering begins with 0.


---

## Next up

